## What each tier buys

A tier fires when the error rate averaged over its long window reaches `burn rate x budget`.
Detection therefore depends on how bad the incident is, while the share of the budget already
spent when the tier fires does not: the error rate cancels out of that expression, leaving
`burn rate x long window / objective window`.

Every figure below is derived for a query engine evaluating a trailing range. A target that
compares period-aligned data points instead — a CloudWatch alarm — cannot fire before the end
of the period the breach fell in, so against that target these are optimistic bounds rather
than equalities.

| Tier | Notifies | Fires at | Budget spent by then | Total outage | 50% errors | At threshold | Clears in |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
@{tier_rows}

The `Total outage` column is also the tier's blind spot, read from the other side: an outage
that ENDS before `threshold x long window` has elapsed never moves the long average far
enough to fire, whatever its severity. The two are one quantity. `Clears in` is measured from
the end of a total outage and is set by the short window; without it the alert would stay
latched for @{worst_latch}.

